1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:05,373
Why Your Bank Pays You Almost Nothing Put
ten thousand dollars in a standard

2
00:00:05,373 --> 00:00:08,013
savings account at one of
America's biggest banks.

3
00:00:08,013 --> 00:00:09,373
Leave it there for a year.

4
00:00:09,373 --> 00:00:11,373
You could earn about one dollar.

5
00:00:11,999 --> 00:00:16,319
That same year, your bank can park money
at the Federal Reserve, overnight,

6
00:00:16,319 --> 00:00:17,519
with zero risk.

7
00:00:17,519 --> 00:00:19,359
And earn three point nine percent.

8
00:00:19,679 --> 00:00:22,560
On ten thousand dollars, that's
three hundred and ninety.

9
00:00:23,683 --> 00:00:26,242
So where do the other three
hundred and eighty-nine dollars

10
00:00:26,722 --> 00:00:27,722
go?

11
00:00:27,282 --> 00:00:29,603
And why do so many people never ask?

12
00:00:29,603 --> 00:00:31,923
This is Billions and Bulls.

13
00:00:31,923 --> 00:00:33,913
Let's follow the money.

14
00:00:34,713 --> 00:00:37,193
Part 1 The One-Dollar Account

15
00:00:37,193 --> 00:00:38,553
Let's start with the numbers.

16
00:00:38,553 --> 00:00:41,023
Because they're stranger than they sound.

17
00:00:41,503 --> 00:00:46,543
In 2026, rate trackers listed the standard
savings accounts at Chase, Bank

18
00:00:46,543 --> 00:00:50,703
of America and Wells Fargo at about
point zero one percent a year.

19
00:00:50,703 --> 00:00:52,314
One hundredth of one percent.

20
00:00:52,314 --> 00:00:55,914
That's ten cents a year for
every thousand dollars.

21
00:00:56,394 --> 00:01:00,267
Across all American banks, it's
better, but not by much.

22
00:01:00,427 --> 00:01:04,747
In October 2026, the national average
savings rate, as published by the

23
00:01:04,747 --> 00:01:09,407
FDIC, the agency that insures bank
deposits, was about point three eight

24
00:01:09,407 --> 00:01:10,407
percent.

25
00:01:10,013 --> 00:01:13,693
On our ten thousand dollars, that's
thirty-eight dollars a year.

26
00:01:13,693 --> 00:01:15,133
Better than one dollar.

27
00:01:15,133 --> 00:01:17,133
Still not much.

28
00:01:17,213 --> 00:01:19,736
Now look at the rate the
whole system runs on.

29
00:01:19,895 --> 00:01:24,055
In September 2026, the Federal Reserve
raised its benchmark rate to a range

30
00:01:24,055 --> 00:01:26,855
of three point seven five to four percent.

31
00:01:26,855 --> 00:01:28,557
In October, it held it there.

32
00:01:28,716 --> 00:01:32,636
And money market funds, which mostly hold
short-term government debt, have

33
00:01:32,636 --> 00:01:34,796
been paying savers close to that rate.

34
00:01:34,796 --> 00:01:38,477
They now hold almost eight
trillion dollars.

35
00:01:38,956 --> 00:01:40,331
And this isn't new.

36
00:01:40,491 --> 00:01:47,211
In 2022 and 2023, the Fed raised rates from
near zero to above five percent,

37
00:01:47,211 --> 00:01:49,531
its fastest climb in decades.

38
00:01:49,531 --> 00:01:51,421
The national average savings rate?

39
00:01:51,421 --> 00:01:55,741
According to FDIC figures, it topped out
at about point four seven percent.

40
00:01:57,261 --> 00:01:59,495
Even some banks pay a lot more.

41
00:01:59,735 --> 00:02:03,735
Online savings accounts, including some
from big banks, have been paying

42
00:02:03,735 --> 00:02:05,255
over three percent.

43
00:02:06,135 --> 00:02:07,975
So picture twenty-five thousand dollars.

44
00:02:08,465 --> 00:02:12,625
At the national average, about
ninety-five dollars a year in interest.

45
00:02:12,625 --> 00:02:16,225
At a top online rate of around three
point seven five percent, according

46
00:02:16,225 --> 00:02:18,545
to rate trackers, more than nine hundred.

47
00:02:18,545 --> 00:02:19,545
Same money.

48
00:02:19,492 --> 00:02:21,332
Same safety, if it's insured.

49
00:02:21,332 --> 00:02:25,253
A difference of about eight hundred
and forty dollars a year.

50
00:02:25,253 --> 00:02:27,092
That gap isn't an accident.

51
00:02:27,092 --> 00:02:28,133
It's a business.

52
00:02:28,133 --> 00:02:33,113
And to understand it, we need to follow
your deposit after you hand it over.

53
00:02:33,833 --> 00:02:36,553
Part 2 Where Your Deposit Goes

54
00:02:36,553 --> 00:02:40,073
When you put money in the bank, it
doesn't sit in a drawer with your name

55
00:02:40,073 --> 00:02:41,073
on it.

56
00:02:40,717 --> 00:02:42,877
Legally, you're lending it to the bank.

57
00:02:42,877 --> 00:02:45,837
The bank owes you the money
back, whenever you ask.

58
00:02:45,837 --> 00:02:49,757
And in the meantime, it puts
that money to work.

59
00:02:49,837 --> 00:02:51,365
Some of it becomes loans.

60
00:02:51,525 --> 00:02:52,525
Mortgages.

61
00:02:52,245 --> 00:02:53,245
Car loans.

62
00:02:53,045 --> 00:02:54,085
Business loans.

63
00:02:54,085 --> 00:02:55,365
And credit cards.

64
00:02:55,365 --> 00:02:58,885
According to Federal Reserve data, the
average interest rate on credit card

65
00:02:58,885 --> 00:03:03,189
accounts that pay interest has been around
twenty-one to twenty-two percent.

66
00:03:03,428 --> 00:03:07,189
Lend our ten thousand dollars on credit
cards at twenty-one percent, and

67
00:03:07,189 --> 00:03:10,068
that's two thousand one hundred
dollars a year in interest.

68
00:03:10,068 --> 00:03:11,748
If the borrowers pay.

69
00:03:11,748 --> 00:03:15,118
Some won't, and the bank has
to cover those losses.

70
00:03:15,518 --> 00:03:19,518
Some of it goes into government bonds,
which pay close to the Fed's rate.

71
00:03:19,518 --> 00:03:22,158
And some of it goes back to the
Federal Reserve itself.

72
00:03:22,158 --> 00:03:26,123
Banks keep reserves there, and the Fed pays
them interest on those reserves.

73
00:03:26,123 --> 00:03:30,523
It only started doing that in 2008,
during the financial crisis.

74
00:03:30,523 --> 00:03:34,202
In the fall of 2026, that rate was
three point nine percent.

75
00:03:35,401 --> 00:03:39,001
The difference between what a bank earns
on its money and what it pays for

76
00:03:39,001 --> 00:03:40,441
it has a name.

77
00:03:40,441 --> 00:03:41,801
Net interest income.

78
00:03:41,801 --> 00:03:44,629
It's the heart of how most
banks make money.

79
00:03:45,108 --> 00:03:50,388
In 2025, JPMorgan Chase, the biggest bank in
the country, reported ninety-five

80
00:03:50,388 --> 00:03:53,908
point four billion dollars in
net interest income.

81
00:03:53,908 --> 00:03:54,963
In one year.

82
00:03:55,123 --> 00:03:59,923
And across all FDIC-insured banks, net
income for 2025 came to two hundred

83
00:03:59,923 --> 00:04:00,923
and ninety-five point six

84
00:04:01,443 --> 00:04:02,563
billion dollars.

85
00:04:02,563 --> 00:04:06,070
Higher interest income was one of
the main reasons it went up.

86
00:04:06,630 --> 00:04:08,950
So deposits are the raw material.

87
00:04:08,950 --> 00:04:11,910
And the cheaper the raw material,
the bigger the margin.

88
00:04:11,910 --> 00:04:13,910
Which raises the obvious question.

89
00:04:13,910 --> 00:04:17,675
If your money is worth so much to the
bank, why doesn't the bank have to

90
00:04:17,675 --> 00:04:19,836
pay more to keep it?

91
00:04:20,475 --> 00:04:22,955
Part 3 The Money That Doesn't Move

92
00:04:22,955 --> 00:04:25,915
The answer is one of the most
valuable things in banking.

93
00:04:25,915 --> 00:04:27,115
Inertia.

94
00:04:27,747 --> 00:04:29,667
Think about your main bank account.

95
00:04:29,667 --> 00:04:31,187
Your salary lands there.

96
00:04:31,187 --> 00:04:32,547
Your bills come out of it.

97
00:04:32,547 --> 00:04:34,228
Your card is linked to it.

98
00:04:34,228 --> 00:04:35,747
Your apps are linked to it.

99
00:04:35,747 --> 00:04:37,747
Moving all that is a chore.

100
00:04:37,747 --> 00:04:38,994
So most people don't.

101
00:04:39,154 --> 00:04:42,354
Even when they could earn
more somewhere else.

102
00:04:42,434 --> 00:04:44,294
Economists have measured this.

103
00:04:44,294 --> 00:04:48,853
In a well-known 2017 study, three
economists at New York University found

104
00:04:48,853 --> 00:04:52,933
that when the Federal Reserve raises
rates, banks pass only part of the

105
00:04:52,933 --> 00:04:54,453
increase on to savers.

106
00:04:54,453 --> 00:04:56,203
They widen the gap instead.

107
00:04:56,363 --> 00:05:00,843
And the gap widens the most in places
where only a few banks compete.

108
00:05:00,843 --> 00:05:04,876
Where customers have fewer places
to go, they get paid less.

109
00:05:05,355 --> 00:05:08,635
People in the industry have a polite
name for this kind of money.

110
00:05:08,635 --> 00:05:09,915
Sticky deposits.

111
00:05:09,915 --> 00:05:13,435
It means money that stays put,
even when the rate is bad.

112
00:05:13,435 --> 00:05:15,858
For a bank, sticky deposits are gold.

113
00:05:16,018 --> 00:05:20,098
They're cheap, they're stable, and they
don't run away when the Fed moves.

114
00:05:20,098 --> 00:05:22,418
Analysts even have a number for it.

115
00:05:22,418 --> 00:05:23,858
The deposit beta.

116
00:05:23,858 --> 00:05:27,831
It measures how much of each Fed rate
rise actually reaches savers.

117
00:05:27,831 --> 00:05:30,711
A beta of one means savers get all of it.

118
00:05:30,711 --> 00:05:34,071
Close to zero means they
get almost nothing.

119
00:05:34,471 --> 00:05:36,272
But inertia has limits.

120
00:05:36,272 --> 00:05:41,352
In the year to June 2023, deposits at
FDIC-insured banks fell four point

121
00:05:41,352 --> 00:05:42,352
eight percent.

122
00:05:42,540 --> 00:05:47,100
The FDIC said it was the first annual
decline in its data going back to

123
00:05:47,100 --> 00:05:48,700
the mid-1990s.

124
00:05:48,700 --> 00:05:52,767
Part of that money went to money market
funds, chasing higher rates.

125
00:05:52,767 --> 00:05:57,487
And when savers get scared instead of just
annoyed, money can move in hours.

126
00:05:57,487 --> 00:06:00,847
That's what happened to Silicon Valley
Bank, in the story we followed in

127
00:06:00,847 --> 00:06:02,900
our Credit Suisse episode.

128
00:06:03,379 --> 00:06:06,579
To be fair, that low rate
isn't pure profit.

129
00:06:06,579 --> 00:06:11,059
A bank account comes with branches, ATMs,
a debit card, fraud protection,

130
00:06:11,059 --> 00:06:13,139
an app, and customer service.

131
00:06:13,139 --> 00:06:14,811
Running all that costs money.

132
00:06:14,971 --> 00:06:18,851
And deposits are insured by the FDIC, up
to two hundred and fifty thousand

133
00:06:18,851 --> 00:06:19,851
dollars

134
00:06:19,611 --> 00:06:22,411
per depositor, per bank, per
account category.

135
00:06:22,411 --> 00:06:24,651
Banks pay for that insurance.

136
00:06:25,293 --> 00:06:28,814
But the gap between one hundredth of a
percent and almost four percent pays

137
00:06:28,814 --> 00:06:30,573
for a lot of ATMs.

138
00:06:31,294 --> 00:06:32,294
Quick thing.

139
00:06:32,173 --> 00:06:34,893
If you like videos that follow
the money, subscribe.

140
00:06:34,893 --> 00:06:36,088
It costs nothing.

141
00:06:36,247 --> 00:06:40,167
Which, for once, is roughly what
your savings account pays.

142
00:06:41,127 --> 00:06:44,087
Part 4 When Saving Paid Less by Law

143
00:06:44,326 --> 00:06:48,487
There was a time when banks paying you
very little wasn't just a choice.

144
00:06:48,487 --> 00:06:50,326
It was the law.

145
00:06:50,726 --> 00:06:55,126
In 1933, in the middle of the Great
Depression, thousands of American banks

146
00:06:55,126 --> 00:06:56,126
had failed.

147
00:06:56,018 --> 00:07:00,259
Lawmakers blamed part of it on banks
competing too hard for deposits, by

148
00:07:00,259 --> 00:07:01,779
promising high rates.

149
00:07:01,779 --> 00:07:05,339
So the Federal Reserve created a
rule called Regulation Q.

150
00:07:05,499 --> 00:07:07,739
It banned interest on checking accounts.

151
00:07:07,739 --> 00:07:11,259
And it set a ceiling on what
banks could pay on savings.

152
00:07:11,820 --> 00:07:14,060
For decades, nobody minded much.

153
00:07:14,060 --> 00:07:15,946
Then came the 1970s.

154
00:07:16,186 --> 00:07:18,826
Inflation climbed into double digits.

155
00:07:18,826 --> 00:07:23,946
In 1979 and 1980, prices rose more
than twelve percent a year.

156
00:07:23,946 --> 00:07:27,889
But savings rates were stuck under the
legal ceiling, around five percent.

157
00:07:28,129 --> 00:07:31,729
Banks couldn't compete on rates,
so they competed on gifts.

158
00:07:31,729 --> 00:07:35,409
Open an account, and some banks would
famously hand you a toaster.

159
00:07:35,409 --> 00:07:39,310
Savers were losing money every
year, just by saving.

160
00:07:39,870 --> 00:07:41,710
So the money found a way out.

161
00:07:41,710 --> 00:07:45,230
In the early 1970s, a new
kind of fund appeared.

162
00:07:45,230 --> 00:07:46,910
The money market fund.

163
00:07:46,910 --> 00:07:49,710
It wasn't a bank, so the
ceiling didn't apply.

164
00:07:49,710 --> 00:07:51,150
It could pay market rates.

165
00:07:51,310 --> 00:07:53,710
And savers poured in.

166
00:07:53,710 --> 00:07:56,510
Banks lost deposits, and
lobbied for change.

167
00:07:56,510 --> 00:08:01,216
In 1980, Congress passed a law to phase
out the ceilings on savings rates.

168
00:08:01,216 --> 00:08:03,775
The last ones were gone by 1986.

169
00:08:03,775 --> 00:08:06,895
The ban on interest for checking
accounts lasted even longer.

170
00:08:06,895 --> 00:08:09,216
It was only lifted in July 2011.

171
00:08:09,216 --> 00:08:11,060
By then, it barely mattered.

172
00:08:11,060 --> 00:08:16,740
From late 2008 to the end of 2015, and
again from 2020 to 2022, the Fed

173
00:08:16,740 --> 00:08:18,199
kept its rate near zero.

174
00:08:18,199 --> 00:08:22,680
When the central bank pays almost nothing,
nobody expects much from a savings

175
00:08:22,680 --> 00:08:23,680
account.

176
00:08:23,399 --> 00:08:26,039
The problem is what happened
when rates came back.

177
00:08:26,039 --> 00:08:28,227
Many savings rates didn't.

178
00:08:28,707 --> 00:08:32,307
So today, no law stops your bank
from paying you more.

179
00:08:32,307 --> 00:08:33,667
It just doesn't have to.

180
00:08:33,667 --> 00:08:37,587
And sometimes, as one bank found out,
keeping that rate low can come with

181
00:08:37,587 --> 00:08:38,947
a price.

182
00:08:39,972 --> 00:08:43,252
Part 5 The Account That Got Left Behind

183
00:08:43,252 --> 00:08:47,968
Capital One had a popular online
account called 360 Savings.

184
00:08:48,128 --> 00:08:52,449
When interest rates started to rise in 2022,
according to the New York Attorney

185
00:08:52,449 --> 00:08:58,384
General, Capital One froze the rate on 360
Savings at point three percent.

186
00:08:58,704 --> 00:09:02,144
It also had a newer account,
launched in 2019.

187
00:09:02,144 --> 00:09:04,624
360 Performance Savings.

188
00:09:04,624 --> 00:09:05,824
Nearly identical.

189
00:09:05,824 --> 00:09:06,824
Same bank.

190
00:09:06,704 --> 00:09:07,704
Same app.

191
00:09:07,504 --> 00:09:09,007
But with a much higher rate.

192
00:09:09,167 --> 00:09:14,127
At one point, the attorney general says,
more than fourteen times higher.

193
00:09:14,527 --> 00:09:18,047
According to the lawsuits, customers
with the old account weren't moved

194
00:09:18,047 --> 00:09:19,047
over.

195
00:09:18,492 --> 00:09:22,811
And according to New York, employees were
told not to bring up the new account,

196
00:09:22,811 --> 00:09:25,771
unless a customer asked about it directly.

197
00:09:26,110 --> 00:09:31,231
In January 2025, the federal
consumer watchdog, the CFPB, sued.

198
00:09:31,231 --> 00:09:34,910
It claimed long-time customers had lost
more than two billion dollars in

199
00:09:34,910 --> 00:09:35,910
interest.

200
00:09:35,696 --> 00:09:39,376
A few weeks later, under new
leadership, the agency dropped the case.

201
00:09:39,376 --> 00:09:43,056
Then, in May 2025, New York
sued on its own.

202
00:09:43,056 --> 00:09:47,362
Capital One said it disagreed with the
claims, and would defend itself.

203
00:09:47,681 --> 00:09:50,641
Meanwhile, customers had
filed a class action.

204
00:09:50,641 --> 00:09:54,881
Capital One agreed to settle it for four
hundred and twenty-five million

205
00:09:54,881 --> 00:09:55,881
dollars.

206
00:09:55,041 --> 00:09:58,924
But in November 2025, a federal
judge rejected the deal.

207
00:09:59,084 --> 00:10:02,524
He wrote that it would pay customers less
than ten percent of the interest

208
00:10:02,524 --> 00:10:03,964
they had actually lost.

209
00:10:03,964 --> 00:10:07,913
And that millions of customers would keep
experiencing, in his words, "the

210
00:10:07,913 --> 00:10:11,994
same financial harm they have already
experienced for years." He also pointed

211
00:10:11,994 --> 00:10:16,314
to an email Capital One had sent, telling
customers about the better account.

212
00:10:16,314 --> 00:10:17,811
Fewer than half opened it.

213
00:10:17,971 --> 00:10:21,571
And only about one percent of the
people who read it did anything.

214
00:10:21,971 --> 00:10:22,971
One percent.

215
00:10:22,931 --> 00:10:25,731
That's inertia, in a single number.

216
00:10:25,891 --> 00:10:29,075
In January 2026, a new deal was announced.

217
00:10:29,315 --> 00:10:32,995
Four hundred and twenty-five million
dollars in restitution, plus a promise

218
00:10:32,995 --> 00:10:37,955
to pay old 360 Savings customers the
same rate as the newer account.

219
00:10:37,955 --> 00:10:39,547
The judge approved it in April.

220
00:10:39,707 --> 00:10:43,467
As this video was made, an appeal was
still delaying the payments.

221
00:10:43,467 --> 00:10:48,027
In early October 2026, Capital One listed
its Performance Savings rate at

222
00:10:48,027 --> 00:10:49,482
three point one percent.

223
00:10:49,722 --> 00:10:53,562
Ten times the old rate that
customers were stuck with.

224
00:10:53,642 --> 00:10:55,965
And Capital One isn't the only case.

225
00:10:55,965 --> 00:11:01,405
In January 2025, the SEC said Wells Fargo
and Merrill Lynch would pay sixty

226
00:11:01,405 --> 00:11:05,405
million dollars over their cash sweep
programs, where clients' idle cash

227
00:11:05,405 --> 00:11:06,534
earned low rates.

228
00:11:06,534 --> 00:11:10,133
The firms neither admitted nor
denied the findings.

229
00:11:11,333 --> 00:11:15,665
Part 6 So, Why Does Your Bank
Pay You Almost Nothing?

230
00:11:15,904 --> 00:11:19,824
Let's go back to our ten thousand
dollars, and follow it one last time.

231
00:11:19,824 --> 00:11:21,264
You hand it to your bank.

232
00:11:21,264 --> 00:11:25,184
The bank lends some of it out, buys
government bonds with some, and parks

233
00:11:25,184 --> 00:11:26,594
some at the Federal Reserve.

234
00:11:26,754 --> 00:11:28,754
Every one of those earns more than you do.

235
00:11:28,754 --> 00:11:30,274
The bank keeps the gap.

236
00:11:30,274 --> 00:11:34,674
And the bigger the gap, and the longer
you stay, the more it keeps.

237
00:11:35,074 --> 00:11:37,692
Your bank pays you about a
dollar a year to hold it.

238
00:11:37,852 --> 00:11:41,292
It can earn close to four hundred on that
same money, without lending it

239
00:11:41,292 --> 00:11:42,292
to anyone.

240
00:11:42,252 --> 00:11:45,236
And a lot more, if it lends it
out on a credit card.

241
00:11:45,396 --> 00:11:49,636
Part of that gap pays for branches, apps,
insurance and the risk of running

242
00:11:49,636 --> 00:11:50,636
a bank.

243
00:11:50,356 --> 00:11:51,796
And part of it is profit.

244
00:11:51,796 --> 00:11:55,386
That's also why banks that don't
have branches often pay more.

245
00:11:55,546 --> 00:12:00,106
They spend less on buildings, and they need
to give people a reason to move.

246
00:12:00,426 --> 00:12:03,386
So, why does your bank pay
you almost nothing?

247
00:12:03,386 --> 00:12:05,466
Not because your money is worth nothing.

248
00:12:05,466 --> 00:12:06,628
It's worth a lot.

249
00:12:06,628 --> 00:12:09,748
It's because the bank knows
most money doesn't move.

250
00:12:09,748 --> 00:12:11,188
Not when rates go up.

251
00:12:11,188 --> 00:12:13,828
Not when a better account
opens at the same bank.

252
00:12:13,828 --> 00:12:16,916
Not even when the bank sends
you an email about it.

253
00:12:17,236 --> 00:12:21,076
The rate on your savings account
isn't a fixed fact of nature.

254
00:12:21,076 --> 00:12:22,116
It's a price.

255
00:12:22,116 --> 00:12:26,379
And like any price, it's set by what
people are willing to accept.

256
00:12:26,699 --> 00:12:29,739
This isn't a recommendation of
any bank or account.

257
00:12:29,739 --> 00:12:31,179
But here's the takeaway.

258
00:12:31,179 --> 00:12:33,019
A savings account is a loan.

259
00:12:33,019 --> 00:12:34,619
From you, to your bank.

260
00:12:34,619 --> 00:12:37,194
The only question is the interest rate.

